 *   reason. A read action may never answer a dialog itself: nothing in this file's read
 *   half touches the browser (`sentinelFieldsFor` is pure - the suite RUNS it with a
 *   poisoned `chrome` to prove that).
 */

//: tabId -> {onEvent, at}: the standing session. Power mode holds one on the working tab.
const sentinels = new Map();
//: The panel dialog policy, cached because the dialog listener is SYNCHRONOUS and cannot
//: await a storage read. Refreshed on every ensureSentinel() (i.e. on every input action)
//: and when a sentinel is created.
let dialogPolicyCache = 'auto-dismiss';
//: tabId -> {state, reason, at}: what the sentinel last said about ITSELF. `aura_status`
//: reads this rather than inferring, so "nobody was watching" is sayable.
const sentinelStateByTab = new Map();
//: tabId -> the last dialog the sentinel saw (see pendingDialogRecord). Keyed by TAB for the
//: same reason the dialog log is: two callers on two tabs must not read each other's dialog.
const pendingDialogByTab = new Map();

/* All three stores are keyed by TAB, so all three die with the tab through the one lifecycle
 * registry (lib/constants.js). `sentinels` was registered when it was born; the two new ones are
 * registered in the same call, because "a cache keyed by a tab id outlives the tab" is the defect
 * that registry exists for (Chrome reuses tab ids). */
onTabClosed('debugger sentinels (sentinels, sentinelStateByTab, pendingDialogByTab)', (id) => {
  if (sentinels.has(id)) releaseSentinel(id, 'tab closed');
  sentinelStateByTab.delete(id);
  pendingDialogByTab.delete(id);
}, () => [...new Set([...sentinels.keys(), ...sentinelStateByTab.keys(), ...pendingDialogByTab.keys()])]);

/* HOW A DIALOG IS ANSWERED, in one place.
 *
 * The same rule was written out three times before this file existed - the event listener, the
 * "a dialog was already open when this action started" probe, and `lib/dialogs.js`'s frozen-
 * renderer recovery - and three copies of one rule is the shape this repo keeps paying for. The
 * rule itself does NOT change here (PROJECT.md: the panel policy decides, not the sentinel):
 *
 *   * the caller's own `dialog={action,text}` wins when there is one - read AT DIALOG TIME,
 *     never captured at attach time (a sentinel lives for the whole power session, so a
 *     captured intent is stale by construction; measured 2026-09-16: a prompt was answered
 *     with the policy, the page logged 名字=null, and the same call had passed before);
 *   * otherwise the PANEL policy picks: `auto-accept` / `auto-accept-prompt` accept,
 *     everything else dismisses - which is what makes `auto-dismiss` the default;
 *   * `defaultPrompt` is the dialog's own default text. `null` means "nothing arrived to
 *     forward" (the pre-existing-dialog probe passes it): Chrome then keeps the dialog's own
 *     default instead of the empty string a guess would type into the page's input.
 *
 * It is PURE, and the suite runs it over inputs, because "the sentinel does not decide to press
 * OK by itself" is exactly the difference between `auto-dismiss` and everything else.
 */
function sentinelAnswerFor(intent, policy, defaultPrompt) {
  const accept = intent ? intent.action === 'accept'
    : (policy === 'auto-accept' || policy === 'auto-accept-prompt');
  // The caller's text wins; else the policy's prompt rule applies; else nothing is sent and
  // Chrome keeps the dialog's own default.
  const promptText = (intent && intent.text !== undefined)
    ? String(intent.text)
    : ((policy === 'auto-accept-prompt' && defaultPrompt !== null)
        ? String(defaultPrompt || '') : undefined);
  return { accept, promptText };
}

/* WHO ANSWERED, AND WHOSE DIALOG IT IS (R29 closure fix). PURE: the intent installed on that tab,
 * its origin, and whether the caller's own dialog command is on it - in; the answer to send and the
 * two fields out - so the suite RUNS it over inputs instead of describing it.
 *
 * WHY IT IS A FUNCTION AND NOT THREE LINES INSIDE THE LISTENER (the defect this replaces): `who`
 * and `owned_by_caller` were read off ONE command-window predicate - `dialogCommandActiveOn(tab)`,
 * "is an aura_dialog command running on this tab" - while `dialog={...}` is installed by EVERY
 * command (background.js's dispatch site). So on the product's OWN recommended path,
 * `aura_click(text="…", dialog={action:"accept", text:"凯"})`, the caller's intent really answered
 * the dialog and the record said `who:"policy"` + `owned_by_caller:false`; and a page timer's
 * dialog opening while an unrelated `aura_dialog` ran was recorded as the caller's. A predicate
 * that answers a DIFFERENT question than the field's name is the A.4.6 shape, and the fix is to
 * read the fact the name promises: the intent that answered, and the command that installed it.
 *
 *   * `intent` / `intent_from` - the caller's `dialog={...}` and the command that installed it
 *     (lib/dialogs.js's `dialogIntentOf` / `dialogIntentSourceOf`).
 *   * `own_command` - a caller's OWN `aura_dialog` command is answering dialogs on that tab, so the
 *     sentinel stands aside instead of racing the caller's answer (measured 2026-09-16).
 *
 * Three answers (A.3.4), and the third one is the point: `who:"caller"` the sentinel sent the
 * caller's intent; `who:"policy"` it sent the panel policy; `who:"none"` it sent nothing because
 * the caller's own command is on the dialog. `owned_by_caller` is NOT `who`: it answers "does this
 * dialog belong to some caller's command" - true when a command installed an intent for this tab,
 * or the caller's own dialog command holds it - so the two can differ (the stand-aside case: the
 * caller's command owns the dialog and the sentinel answered nothing). */
function sentinelDialogDecision(ctx) {
  const c = ctx || {};
  const intent = c.intent || null;
  const from = c.intent_from || null;
  // THE ORIGIN IS WHAT MAKES IT THE CALLER'S DIALOG: an intent no command installed is not
  // something any caller declared, and answering it as theirs would be the same defect wearing a
  // different name.
  const owned = !!(intent && from);
  if (c.own_command) {
    return {
      answer: null, who: 'none', owned_by_caller: true,
      why: "the caller's own dialog command is answering the dialogs on this tab, so the standing"
         + ' policy stays out of the way: the caller owns this dialog and this record claims no answer',
    };
  }
  const answer = sentinelAnswerFor(owned ? intent : null, c.policy, c.default_prompt);
  return {
    answer: answer,
    who: owned ? 'caller' : 'policy',
    owned_by_caller: owned,
    why: owned
      ? "the caller's own dialog={…} was installed by " + String((from && from.action) || 'a command')
      : 'no caller declared an intent for this tab, so the panel policy answered',
  };
}

/* ONE dialog, as the record a caller later reads. PURE: the CDP event plus what was known at
 * that moment in, the reported record out
 * describing it, and so `aura_status` cannot answer with a shape nothing produces.
 *
 * TWO QUESTIONS, TWO FIELDS (R29 batch 5b). This record used to carry ONE field,
 * `action_in_flight`, that could not answer its own name: it was `mine || laneBusy(tab)`, so a
 * dialog the PAGE raised was reported "in flight" whenever any command occupied that tab's lane -
 * and "nobody owns this" is the fact the record exists for (measured: `tools/run_zoo.py s13
 * --power` read `action_in_flight: True` on a dialog whose `who` said `policy`). Each field now
 * answers one question, and its name says which:
 *
 *   * `lane_busy` - was an action occupying that tab's lane at that moment? A FACT, and it may be
 *     true of a dialog nobody owns (the call site says what it counts);
 *   * `owned_by_caller` - is this dialog owned by some caller's command? Its OWN input, decided in
 *     `sentinelDialogDecision` (an intent a command installed for this tab, or the caller's own
 *     dialog command holding it) - NOT a reading of `who`, so the two CAN differ: while a caller's
 *     own command is answering, the dialog is the caller's (`true`) and this sentinel sent nothing
 *     (`who:'none'`). Reading it off a command-window predicate for both fields is the defect the
 *     R29 closure fix removes.
 *
 * `who`/`accepted` record the DECISION, `delivered` records whether Chrome took it: it stays `null`
 * until the send settles, because claiming an answer before the protocol confirmed it is the A.3.4
 * defect itself.
 */
function pendingDialogRecord(params, ctx) {
  const p = params || {};
  const c = ctx || {};
  const byPolicy = c.who === 'policy';
  const byCaller = c.who === 'caller';
  return {
    type: String(p.type || ''),
    message: String(p.message || '').slice(0, 200),
    frame_id: p.frameId == null ? null : Number(p.frameId),
    at: Number(c.at || 0),
    lane_busy: !!c.lane_busy,
    // ITS OWN INPUT (R29 closure fix): it used to be `who === 'caller'`, which made two fields out
    // of one fact and let a command-window predicate decide both. The producer
    // (`sentinelDialogDecision`) passes what the name asks for; a caller that passes only `who`
    // (the node tables, and any older sender) reads as before - a default, not a second truth.
    owned_by_caller: c.owned_by_caller === undefined ? byCaller : !!c.owned_by_caller,
    policy: String(c.policy || ''),
    who: byPolicy ? 'policy' : (byCaller ? 'caller' : 'none'),
    // WHAT THIS RECORD CLAIMS TO HAVE SENT. Only an answer THIS file sent is claimed: when the
    // caller's own command is answering it (`who:'none'`), claiming what it chose would report a
    // decision this file never saw. `null` says "answered elsewhere, and I do not report it".
    accepted: (byPolicy || byCaller) ? !!c.accept : null,
    delivered: null,
    error: '',
  };
}

function notePendingDialog(tabId, params, ctx) {
  const key = dialogKey(tabId);
  if (key == null) return null;
  const rec = pendingDialogRecord(params, ctx);
  pendingDialogByTab.set(key, rec);
  return rec;
}

function pendingDialogFor(tabId) {
  const key = dialogKey(tabId);
  return key == null ? null : (pendingDialogByTab.get(key) || null);
}

function noteSentinelState(tabId, state, reason) {
  const key = dialogKey(tabId);
  if (key == null) return;
  sentinelStateByTab.set(key, { state: String(state), reason: String(reason || ''), at: Date.now() });
}

/* IS ANYBODY WATCHING THIS TAB? The three answers (A.3.4), and the third one is the point:
 *
 *   on       - a standing session is attached, as far as this worker knows. Chrome can drop one
 *              without telling us (that is what `forgetSentinel` is for), and a read action may
 *              not spend a CDP call to find out - so this says what the WORKER holds, not what
 *              Chrome is doing right now.
 *   off      - nothing is attached and the reason is known: power mode is off, the attach was
 *              refused (DevTools on that tab, say), the tab closed, or this caller has no
 *              working tab yet.
 *   unknown  - the last attach ran out of time. Whether a session exists is then not knowable
 *              from here, and `sentinelStatusFor` refuses to answer "off" on a guess.
 */
function sentinelStatusFor(tabId, settings) {
  const key = dialogKey(tabId);
  const policy = dialogPolicyCache;
  if (key == null) {
    return { state: 'off', tab: null, reason: 'this caller has no working tab yet, so no tab is being watched', policy, at: null };
  }
  if (sentinels.has(key)) {
    const w = sentinels.get(key) || {};
    return { state: 'on', tab: key, reason: '', policy, at: Number(w.at || 0) || null };
  }
  if (!settings || !settings.powerMode) {
    return { state: 'off', tab: key, reason: 'power mode is off: nothing is attached between actions', policy, at: null };
  }
  const last = sentinelStateByTab.get(key) || null;
  if (last && last.state === 'unknown') {
    return { state: 'unknown', tab: key, reason: last.reason, policy, at: last.at };
  }
  return {
    state: 'off', tab: key, policy, at: last ? last.at : null,
    reason: (last && last.reason) || 'no input action has attached a standing session to this tab yet',
  };
}

/* WHAT `aura_status` ADDS (R29 batch 5): the two fields, built in ONE place so the reader's
 * question ("what did I miss between actions, and is anybody watching?") has one answer.
 *
 * A READ PATH, and nothing in it may touch the browser: no attach, no `handleJavaScriptDialog`,
 * no CDP call. The recorder above writes these two maps; this only reads them. The suite runs
 * this function with a `chrome` proxy that throws on any property access.
 */
function sentinelFieldsFor(tabId, settings) {
  return {
    pending_dialog: pendingDialogFor(tabId),
    sentinel: sentinelStatusFor(tabId, settings),
  };
}

/* Everything that answers a dialog, in one place. */
async function attachWatcher(tabId) {
  const target = { tabId };
  try {
    await chrome.debugger.attach(target, '1.3');
  } catch (e) {
    // DevTools being open on that tab is the common cause; make it actionable.
    throw new Error(
      'power mode needs the DevTools protocol, but Chrome refused to attach' +
        ' (close DevTools / another debugger on that tab and retry): ' +
        (e && e.message ? e.message : String(e)),
    );
  }
  const s = await getSettings();
  const policy = String(s.dialogPolicy || 'auto-dismiss');
  // The panel policy is cached because a dialog listener is synchronous and cannot await
  // a storage read. It is refreshed on every ensureSentinel() (i.e. on every input
  // action) and when the sentinel is created, so it is never far behind.
  dialogPolicyCache = policy;
  /* The per-call intent and the policy are read AT DIALOG TIME, not here.
   *
   * This is the bug the sentinel introduced and this line fixes: when a session lived for
   * exactly one command, capturing the intent at attach time was harmless (the capture was
   * as fresh as the command). A sentinel lives for the whole power-mode session, so a
   * captured intent is stale by definition - measured 2026-09-16: with the sentinel in
   * place, `aura_click(text="弹个 prompt", dialog={action:accept,text:凯})` answered the
   * prompt with the POLICY (dismiss) and the page logged `名字=null`, while the same call
   * passed before the sentinel existed. */
  const onEvent = (source, method, params) => {
    if (!source || source.tabId !== tabId) return;
    if (method !== 'Page.javascriptDialogOpening') return;
    dialogLogFor(tabId).push({ type: params.type, message: params.message || '', defaultPrompt: params.defaultPrompt || '' });
    pushTabEvent('dialog', { tabId: source.tabId, type: params.type, message: String(params.message || '').slice(0, 160) });
    /* ONE DECISION, USED FOR THE ANSWER AND FOR THE RECORD (R29 closure fix). The facts it reads
     * are the INTENT that would answer this dialog and the command that installed it - not "is an
     * aura_dialog command running", which answered a different question than the field's name (see
     * `sentinelDialogDecision`). Two calls could disagree, and then the status would report an
     * answer the page never received.
     *
     * `own_command` is the one thing the command window is still for, and only for the ANSWER: the
     * caller's own `aura_dialog` must not race the standing policy (per TAB - with two tabs
     * answering at once a single slot let the policy dismiss the dialog the other caller was
     * answering; see lib/dialogs.js).
     *
     * `lane_busy` is a THIRD fact and is not either of the two fields: the caller's own `aura_status`
     * read is exempt from the lane (lib/lanes.js runs it inline), but every OTHER scheduled action
     * counts - including read-only ones (`aura_read`, `aura_snapshot`, ...) and a navigation still
     * waiting on the page - so `lane_busy: true` beside `owned_by_caller: false` is the NORMAL
     * reading for a page timer's dialog. HONEST LIMIT: it counts read-only occupancy too, so it is a
     * fact about the SCHEDULE, not about the page being changed. */
    const decision = sentinelDialogDecision({
      intent: dialogIntentOf(tabId),
      intent_from: dialogIntentSourceOf(tabId),
      own_command: dialogCommandActiveOn(source.tabId),
      policy: dialogPolicyCache,
      default_prompt: params.defaultPrompt == null ? '' : params.defaultPrompt,
    });
    const answer = decision.answer;
    const rec = notePendingDialog(tabId, params, {
      at: Date.now(),
      lane_busy: laneBusy(source.tabId),
      policy: dialogPolicyCache,
      who: decision.who,
      owned_by_caller: decision.owned_by_caller,
      accept: answer ? answer.accept : false,
    });
    if (!answer) return;
    // The dialog cannot outlive this debugger session (Chrome drops it on detach), so it is
    // always answered here and reported in the result. `delivered` starts null and is filled in
    // by whichever way the send settles: an answer claimed before Chrome confirmed it would be
    // the same defect as `dialog_confirmed`'s reversed default (A.5).
    chrome.debugger
      .sendCommand(target, 'Page.handleJavaScriptDialog', answer)
      .then(() => { if (rec) rec.delivered = true; },
            (e) => {
              if (!rec) return;
              rec.delivered = false;
              rec.error = String((e && e.message) || e).slice(0, 120);
            });
  };
  chrome.debugger.onEvent.addListener(onEvent);
  // THE LISTENER IS ONLY ARMED IF THIS SUCCEEDS (audit finding, 2026-09-20): `Page.enable` is what
  // makes `Page.javascriptDialogOpening` arrive at all, and swallowing its failure let
  // `ensureSentinel` register a sentinel and report `sentinel_attached` while an unattended dialog
  // would never reach `onEvent` - the exact failure the sentinel exists to prevent, wearing the
  // badge of the fix. Throwing routes it into `ensureSentinel`'s catch, which pushes
  // `sentinel_attach_failed` WITH the reason and returns false; that path already detaches, so
  // "no sentinel" and "no session" stay the same fact. (The OTHER `Page.enable` in
  // `lib/dialogs.js:clearStuckDialog` keeps its empty catch on purpose: it is a warm-up in front of
  // an answer whose own failure IS reported, so it cannot create a false claim.)
  try {
    await chrome.debugger.sendCommand(target, 'Page.enable', {});
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('Page.enable failed, so no dialog event can arrive: ' +
                    String((e && e.message) || e).slice(0, 120));
  }
  // A dialog opened while NO command was running (page timer, websocket callback)
  // has nobody to answer it: the renderer stays blocked and the next command
  // stalls (measured: a plain aura_read took 24.5s and nearly hit its timeout).
  // Probing costs one CDP call; it throws when no dialog is open, which is normal.
  try {
    // The intent is read HERE, at dialog time, exactly as `onEvent` does - not from a variable
    // captured at attach time (there is none), and not from the one that used to be named here:
    // measured 2026-09-17, this line read an identifier that is declared nowhere in the worker, so
    // evaluating the argument threw a ReferenceError, the `catch` below swallowed it as "nothing was
    // waiting", and the pre-existing-dialog branch NEVER RAN - neither the answer nor the log entry
    // it exists for. `node --check` cannot see this: it is a runtime read, not a syntax error.
    const preIntent = dialogIntentOf(tabId);
    const prePolicy = dialogPolicyCache;
    await chrome.debugger.sendCommand(target, 'Page.handleJavaScriptDialog',
                                      sentinelAnswerFor(preIntent, prePolicy, null));
    dialogLogFor(tabId).push({ type: 'pre-existing', message: 'a dialog was already open when this action started (answered by policy)', defaultPrompt: '' });
  } catch (e) { /* nothing was waiting - the usual case */ }
  return { onEvent, at: Date.now() };
}

/* Forget a sentinel Chrome has already dropped.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS (measured 2026-09-16, Chrome's own words in an E2E run):
 *
 *     aura_type -> extension error: Debugger is not attached to the tab with id: 1576582111
 *
 * ...and every action after it said the same thing. Chrome can drop a debugger session
 * without telling us (the tab was replaced, another client took it, an attach was
 * cancelled), and this Map then still claimed we held one - so `cdpSession` "borrowed" a
 * session that was not there and every command failed with a raw English protocol error
 * that no caller in this system knows how to act on.
 *
 * `releaseSentinel` cannot be used here: it calls `chrome.debugger.detach`, which is the
 * wrong move for a session Chrome has already detached. This only forgets.
 */
/* THE ONE PLACE A STANDING SESSION STOPS BEING ONE (R29 closure fix).
 *
 * WHY IT IS ONE FUNCTION (the defect this fixes): three paths lose a session - Chrome detaching
 * (`chrome.debugger.onDetach`, which is how a [取消] on the infobar and a DevTools takeover arrive),
 * a CDP budget expiry (background.js's `cdpRun` detaches to free a jammed lane), and a protocol
 * error saying the session is gone (`SESSION_GONE`, below). Only the third used to clean up, so
 * after the first two `aura_status` kept answering `sentinel.state:"on"` with no reason while
 * `pending_dialog: null` quietly meant BOTH "no dialog" and "nobody is watching" - and the network
 * rules that session was carrying were still declared as intercepting. Three copies of one cleanup
 * is how that happened, so every path now calls THIS.
 *
 * It runs even when the map holds no entry (unlike the old `if (!w) return false`): the network
 * lane's armed flag can outlive the entry it was derived from, and "the session is gone" is a fact
 * about the TAB, not about the map. `releaseSentinel` is the other end - a session WE end on
 * purpose - and goes through the same tail. */
function closeStandingSession(tabId, why, event) {
  const w = sentinels.get(tabId);
  if (w) {
    sentinels.delete(tabId);
    try { chrome.debugger.onEvent.removeListener(w.onEvent); } catch (e) {}
  }
  // R29 batch 6: the session that carried the network rules is gone, so lib/net.js's rules are no
  // longer intercepting. They stay in the table (only the caller's own undo removes them) and are
  // reported as inactive instead of being counted as interception - and the Network domain is
  // forgotten with the session, so the next attach re-arms it.
  netDisarm(tabId, why);
  noteSentinelState(tabId, 'off', why);
  pushTabEvent(event || 'sentinel_forgotten', { tabId, why: String(why || '') });
  return !!w;
}

function forgetSentinel(tabId, why) {
  return closeStandingSession(
    tabId,
    String(why || 'Chrome dropped the session this worker held')
      + '; the next input action attaches a new one');
}

//: Chrome's wordings for "the session you think you have is gone". Matched on the protocol
//: error, not on our own prose, because this is about what CHROME said.
const SESSION_GONE = /not attached to the tab|No tab with given id|Cannot access|Detached while handling/i;

async function releaseSentinel(tabId, why) {
  /* A SESSION WE END ON PURPOSE, through the same tail as one Chrome took away: the registration,
   * the network lane's claim on it, the state note and the event are one fact, so they are written
   * once (`closeStandingSession`). This function only adds the detach - and for a session Chrome has
   * already dropped that is a no-op behind a catch, which is why `forgetSentinel` cannot call it. */
  const had = closeStandingSession(tabId, 'no standing session: ' + String(why || 'released'),
                                   'sentinel_released');
  try { await chrome.debugger.detach({ tabId }); } catch (e) {}
  return had;
}

async function ensureSentinel(tabId) {
  if (tabId == null) return false;
  const s = await getSettings();
  // keep the listener's cached policy fresh without a storage read inside a sync callback
  dialogPolicyCache = String(s.dialogPolicy || 'auto-dismiss');
  if (!s.powerMode) {
    // power mode was turned off while we held a session: let it go
    if (sentinels.has(tabId)) await releaseSentinel(tabId, 'power mode off');
    return false;
  }
  if (sentinels.has(tabId)) return true;
  // Bounded, and this is not decoration: attachWatcher ends with `Page.enable`, which was
  // MEASURED to time out against a renderer already blocked by a dialog. Unbounded, this
  // call would hang ensureInputReady - and therefore every input action - forever, which
  // is the very failure the sentinel exists to prevent.
  let w = null;
 * rules that session was carrying were still declared as intercepting. Three copies of one cleanup
 * is how that happened, so every path now calls THIS.
 *
 * It runs even when the map holds no entry (unlike the old `if (!w) return false`): the network
 * lane's armed flag can outlive the entry it was derived from, and "the session is gone" is a fact
 * about the TAB, not about the map. `releaseSentinel` is the other end - a session WE end on
 * purpose - and goes through the same tail. */
function closeStandingSession(tabId, why, event) {
  const w = sentinels.get(tabId);
  if (w) {
    sentinels.delete(tabId);
    try { chrome.debugger.onEvent.removeListener(w.onEvent); } catch (e) {}
  }
  // R29 batch 6: the session that carried the network rules is gone, so lib/net.js's rules are no
  // longer intercepting. They stay in the table (only the caller's own undo removes them) and are
  // reported as inactive instead of being counted as interception - and the Network domain is
  // forgotten with the session, so the next attach re-arms it.
  netDisarm(tabId, why);
  // ...AND THE DIALOG THE SESSION WAS HOLDING: Chrome drops it on detach, so this record would be a
  // report about a dialog nobody can be looking at (see forgetPendingDialog).
  forgetPendingDialog(tabId);
  noteSentinelState(tabId, 'off', why);
  pushTabEvent(event || 'sentinel_forgotten', { tabId, why: String(why || '') });
  if (!w) {
    try { await chrome.debugger.detach({ tabId }); } catch (e) {}
    return false;
  }
  sentinels.set(tabId, w);
  noteSentinelState(tabId, 'on', '');
  pushTabEvent('sentinel_attached', { tabId });
  /* THE NETWORK DOMAIN IS ARMED WITH THE SESSION, NOT WITH THE FIRST NETWORK VERB (R29 closure fix).
   *
   * `Network.enable` used to be sent lazily by `netStartSession` - i.e. by `aura_net_log` /
   * `aura_net_block` / `aura_net_mock` - so a request the page made BEFORE the first of those calls
   * was never recorded, and `aura_net_log` answered `count: 0` for it: "this page made no requests"
   * instead of "nobody was listening yet" (measured: the s34 arena scenario read `seen: 0` while the
   * fixture server had counted the request the page made). Listening is a property of the session,
   * and lib/net.js re-arms on every attach because the domain belongs to the SESSION - `netDisarm`
   * clears it when one ends. */
  try {
    await netArmSession(tabId);
  } catch (e) {
    // NOT SILENT AND NOT FATAL: the sentinel works without the network lane and the next network
    // verb retries the arming - but the reason goes in the ring instead of into a swallowed error.
    pushTabEvent('net_enable_failed', { tabId, why: String((e && e.message) || e).slice(0, 160) });
  }
  return true;
}

